Regarding the affair of irregularities and fraud in the recruitment competition for the Ministry of the Economy and Finance, the reaction of the political class (the opposition) was awaited, and now we have it.

This latest scandal of the Yayi regime has broken the silence of one of Benin's few committed women politicians, Celestine Zanou. Instead of a press conference, a television interview or a press release, the president of the Dynamique du Changement pour un Benin debout has chosen to address herself directly to President Boni Yayi, who has in fact remained inactive since this affair broke out - the second scandal of his reign to directly affect the people, after the ICC Services and associates affair. In her open letter, which we publish below in full, with a continuation on page 12, Celestine Zanou hopes that Boni Yayi will take "the appropriate measures", "with the sole aim of restoring the reputation of the Public Service and giving back hope to the young people who, with this competition, no longer believe in equality of opportunity in their country." Read on.

Cotonou, 26 February 2013

Subject: Re: Recruitment competition for the MEF

Mr President of the Republic,

Open letter to Mr Boni YAYI, President of the Republic, Head of State, Head of Government
Cotonou

Once again Duty calls me and, as is my habit, I find it impossible to resist, despite my serious current preoccupations. That is to say that I am definitively convinced that for battles that involve the future of my country, of the Beninese people and of its disoriented and humiliated youth (who must content themselves with footballs for lack of jobs), no sacrifice is too great. I submit to it with the hope of finally being heard by you, or failing that, of having had the merit of sounding the alarm in the face of these evils that gnaw, under your reign, at the Beninese people in their numerical majority.

From the memorable electoral knockout, our country is inexorably settling into a social, economic, moral and political chaos that even supporters of your regime, and not the least of them, acknowledge in private. It is a red alert everywhere, and for good reason:

- the unequal social treatment that I denounced in 2009 in a pamphlet entitled "The 10 SETBACKS of Change", which I sent to you at the time, has been amplified against a background of nepotism, favouritism and above all a regionalism never experienced before, a glaring and outrageous regionalism that takes your breath away and that lies at the heart of this famous competition, now the administrative scandal of the year!!!

- Haphazard governance against a background of dictatorship that is destroying by great fire our dearly acquired democracy, with a suspension of any sense of the State and of values, confirmed by monumental state errors unworthy of our country and its people, whose living elite - and even those departed - shake their heads.

- a management of people made of contempt and lack of respect for elders, which moreover instils fear in hearts, forgetting the natural principle that when fear (it) changes sides, nothing can stop the oppressed.

Mr President of the Republic, this country has a soul. Stop continuing to torture it, to spare this peaceful people that which I have no wish to name here and now.

Committed I am, to the cause of Benin, of all of Benin, one and indivisible. I think I am so in the same way as you, in any case in the same way as other compatriots who at this moment have a throat tied and tight, like a cancer gnawing at its bearer. But unlike my brothers and sisters with tight throats, and faced with these numerous scandals that debase our country and tarnish its image beyond our borders, I have made the responsible choice to externalise my moral suffering by addressing myself directly to your High Person.

Yes, Mr President of the Republic, I am in pain, and many of us have been in this state for some years and even more so for the last two months, faced with this murky affair that the competition for the recruitment of Permanent State Agents for the Ministry of the Economy and Finance (MEF) has become.

Mr President of the Republic, allow me to remind you of a few extracts from my address of 31 March 2011, the substance of which escaped many Beninese and, it seems to me, yourself. These are the following passages:

- "Mr President of the Republic, if your first term is perfectible, the second must come close to perfect, because you will no longer have the excuse of not knowing the house well."

- "The term that is beginning will be one of all challenges. I foresee it as difficult for several reasons... The delicate election we are emerging from requires a permanent approach of preserving harmony. Internally the challenges are already enormous and underestimating them could be serious and irreversible, because today it is a matter of having, at all levels, an approach of sincerity towards the people. It is fundamentally a matter of seeking a perfect accord between word and deed. Be, as you proclaim, the President of all Beninese."

- "Thus, to better succeed in your five-year term, I advise you to beware of the incompetent and of the time-gnawers who did nothing to make your first term shine. You should also be wary of the arrogant people around you who do nothing to project a new image of your person, still less to soothe hearts. From the ordeal, let us draw the resources to grow..."

With this competition for Permanent State Agents for the MEF, are we really within the logic I advised you of on 31 March 2011? I will not take refuge behind any false modesty in order not to affirm here that, had it not been for my message, you would doubtless have had difficulty being sworn in in 2011, so strong was the tension!!! Our main southern towns militarised, political opponents ready for anything, believing their victory had been stolen. May amnesia not overtake you because of the new political developments and unnatural realignments "favourable" to your camp or to your person.

Mr President of the Republic, to keep silent in the face of this scandalous competition is suicidal and history will remind everyone of it in the very near future.

That is why I have resolved to break my own silence to respectfully challenge you on the ins and outs of this file, whose stench has crossed not only our borders but also and above all the limits of the acceptable, painting our country in a colour that hardly honours it. Yes, I challenge you, Mr President, because you do not have the right to continue to remain indifferent as is currently the case. Do you really need this curious commission of inquiry chaired by your famous Special Adviser for Political Affairs to understand this self-evident file, with admissions as important as those of your Minister, who wrote in black and white that her collaborators falsified a communique without her knowledge? No, no and no!!! Your already too heavy silence could be labelled complicit if it persisted, and would thus prove right all those commentators and other victims I have had the opportunity to listen to. Mr President of the Republic, allow me to recall a few facts for your enlightenment, in case the reports you receive from your collaborators have been deliberately truncated.

Your Minister of Labour and the Public Service, Madam Memouna Kora Zaki, deliberately turned a deaf ear to all proposals aimed at protecting our country from this scandal, as if she insisted at all costs on rolling out an orchestrated plan, and her serenity in the face of all the denunciations leaves the field open to dangerous interpretations, destructive of social cohesion and peace. Thus she:

- royally trivialised and ignored the alert from the officials of FESYNTRA-FINANCES who, having learned that the results of this competition were known in advance, had made concrete proposals aimed at guaranteeing a certain transparency of the said competition (see annex 1, the communique of 16 July 2012 from FESYNTRA-FINANCES concerning the recruitment competitions, and annex 2, a copy of letter no. 020/FESYNTRA-FINANCES/SG of 17 July 2012 to the Prime Minister);

- categorically refused to accede to the transparency proposals of FESYNTRA-FINANCES;

- actively participated in the flagrant violation of the provisions of article 23 of law 86-013 by adopting, jointly with the Minister of the Economy and Finance, an interministerial order on the eve of the competition, an order that was supposed to be taken four months before the competitions were held (see annex 3, copies of the two orders signed on 27 July 2012 for competitions starting on 28 July 2012).

This attitude of your Minister led FESYNTRA-FINANCES to abstain from any participation in the work of the various commissions, removing all credibility from the entire process (see annex 4, the position of FESYNTRA-FINANCES on the organisation of the recruitment competitions for 28 July 2012, a position made public on 27 July 2012).

Despite this, the competition was organised against all odds with an amateurism like no other and with irregularities unworthy of a State of law in the 21st century, which illustrate perfectly the advanced dismantling of our heavily politicised Administration. FESYNTRA-FINANCES had at the time asked you for the pure and simple cancellation of the said competition (see annex 5, a copy of letter no. 023/FESYNTRA-FINANCES/SG that FESYNTRA-FINANCES addressed to your Authority on 14 August 2012).

Consider:

- The Minister of Labour and the Public Service took the liberty of violating interministerial order no. 577/MTFP/MEF/DC/SGMDGFP/DRAESTCD/SA of 27 July 2012, taken jointly with her colleague of Finance, by appointing to the various juries more people than provided for (see annex 6, a copy of interministerial order no. 577/MTFP/MEF/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAESTCD/SA of 27 July 2012 and annex 7, a copy of Decision no. 929/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 31 December 2012 constituting the jury responsible for the deliberation of the competitions for the recruitment of Permanent State Agents for the Administration of Taxes, the Treasury, Customs and Indirect Duties, sessions of 28 July and 25 August 2012).

- The Director General of the Public Service took the liberty of issuing a service note to complete with impunity Decision no. 672/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 7 September 2012 constituting the anonymity jury in the framework of the competitions for the recruitment of Permanent State Agents for the Administration of Taxes, the Treasury, Customs and Indirect Duties, sessions of 28 July and 25 August 2012, thus violating the elementary rules of the Administration; and all this without any reaction from his hierarchy (see annex 8, a copy of Decision no. 672/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 7 September 2012 and annex 9, a copy of service note no. 216/MTFP/DGFP/SA of 2 October 2012).

- the organisers of the competition, notably the DRAE (a contract agent eventually raised to the rank of head of recruitment of State agents), reportedly did not hesitate to associate candidates with the organisation of the competitions, privileged candidates naturally admitted!!! What country are we in? The most illustrative case is said to be that of Mr Garba Asso Aziz, a direct relative of the DRAE. What audacity? What an insult? What effrontery? My country cannot come to this and I appeal to your honour to restore the sense of the State, the sense of proper conduct and the sense of fairness.

- inadmissible errors in certain examinations, notably in accounting, made them impossible to complete for "normal" candidates while the others were able to handle the paper with the error; did these have a privilege somewhere? The question remains posed.

- The unsecured transport of candidates' scripts to secret destinations;

- Competition held on 25 July and 28 August 2012 and the marking jury set up on 25 October 2012; what does this delay answer to? Where were the scripts and what was being done with them? (see annex 10, a copy of Decision no. 824/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 25 October 2012 constituting the marking jury in the framework of the competitions for the recruitment of permanent State agents for the administrations of the treasury, taxes and estates, sessions of 28 July and 25 August 2012);

- By what alchemy could the deliberation of the competition take place on 31 December 2012, the date of adoption of the interministerial order establishing the members of the deliberation jury? (see annex 11 = annex 7)

- How can a communique signed by the same Minister, on the same day, with the same registration number, have different contents and different initials? Either the amateurism is at its peak, or the muddle ended up muddling its own authors, and these are obvious signs of characterised administrative incompetence. (see annex 12, a copy of the first version of communique no. 49/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 31 December 2012 and annex 13, a copy of the second version of communique no. 49/MTFP/DC/SGM/DGFP/DRAE/STCD/SA of 31 December 2012). In any case it is thanks to this communique that Pandora's box was opened and the whole people, at home and abroad, was informed of what had been plotted.

Indeed, thanks to the famous communique and to the complaint of Mrs Idjouola Agbeke Yemangnisse Lydie, the whole world is aware of the deception, the serious irregularities, the fraudulent manoeuvres, the favouritism and the discrimination that tainted this competition from its preparation to the proclamation of the pseudo-results. It was thus revealed in broad daylight that:

- The DGFP and the DRAE did (according to the Minister's own words) surreptitiously falsify the communique in order to fraudulently insert another name in place of Mrs Idjouola (see annex 14, a copy of letter no. 012/MTFP/SP-C of 14 January 2013 from the MTFP to the President of the OLC);

- many of those admitted to this competition are of the lineage of the organisers of the competition, as attested by the various tables published by FESYNTRA-FINANCES without the slightest denial to this day (see annex 15, a copy of the FESYNTRA-FINANCES publication dated 29 January 2013 and annex 16, a copy of the FESYNTRA-FINANCES publication dated 31 January 2013);

- some candidates were able to hand in a script conforming to the erroneous model answer, which justified a responsible reaction from a jury President;

- the Director General of the Public Service and the Director of Recruitment of State Agents are not unanimous on the place where candidates' scripts were marked. For the former, the marking took place at the Ministry of Labour and the Public Service, and for the latter it took place at the Technical Lycee (without further detail) for the large corps and at the Directorate (without further detail) for certain corps with reduced numbers (see annex 17, a copy of the OLC questionnaire completed by the DGFP on 16 January 2013, annex 18, a copy of the same questionnaire completed by the DRAE on the same day, and annex 19, a summary table drawn up by myself presenting side by side the respective answers of the DGFP and the DRAE to each OLC question);

- The Minister of Labour and the Public Service, who claims to have nothing personally to reproach herself with in this file, nevertheless refused the OLC access to the candidates' scripts, thus violating decree no. 2008-180 of 8 April 2008 creating the Observatory for the Fight against Corruption and setting out its powers, organisation and functioning (see annex 20, a copy of letter no. 052/OLC/PT/SP/SA/2013 of 17 January 2013 from the President of the OLC and annex 21, a copy of letter 25/MTFP/SP-C dated 17 January 2013 from the Minister of Labour and the Public Service).

So many irregularities noted by FESYNTRA-FINANCES, the OLC and the Press, and relayed on social networks, cannot wall you up in silence while awaiting the results of a commission of inquiry. Your silence to this day worries me, Mr President of the Republic!!!

- You who have a government charter in which responsibility and transparency are clearly displayed!!!

- You who, on the afternoon of 17 July 2007, organised a green march against corruption to signify your determination to fight corruption, bad governance, waste and impunity!!!

- You who have made the obligation of results (good results, I presume) and accountability your governance creed!!!

Mr President of the Republic, may you recall that for less than this in this country you have dismissed officials at various levels, even members of the government, without it being necessary for you to create a commission of inquiry. I mean to name:

- Mr Gaston Dossouhoui, your Minister of Agriculture of 2006
- Mr Alexandre Dossou Kpedetin, your Minister of Public Works of 2006;
- Mrs Colette HOUETO, your Minister of Primary and Secondary Education of 2006;
- Mr Armand ZINZINDOHOUE, your Minister of the Interior of 2010;
- Mr Soule Mana LAWANI, your Minister of Finance of 2010;
- Mr Celestin EZIN, the Regional Director of SBEE Littoral in 2006;
- Mrs Martine AHOUNOU GOMEZ, former Director of the Palais des Congres of Cotonou;
- Mr Moudachirou BACHABI, former Director of New Works at the Ministry of Public Works in 2006;
- Mr Paul Dassi HOUNHOUI, former Director General of Public Works in 2006;
- Mr Severin EBLOHOUE, former Director General of the National Centre for Testing and Research in Public Works;
- Mr Epiphane NOMBIME, former Secretary General of the Government;
- Mr Constant AMOUSSOU, former Attorney General;
- Mr Honorat HESSOU, former Prefect of the Atlantique and Littoral;
- Mr Joseph AHANHANZO GLELE, former Director General of the Autonomous Port of Cotonou;
- Mr Edouard OUIN OURO, former Secretary General of the Presidency of the Republic;
- Etc.

The list is long and reinforces my questioning as to the management of people under your Presidency. I have served at the highest level and, with full knowledge, I wonder whether Benin is henceforth run at two speeds. Might there be privileged people to whom everything is permitted, including offences, and non-privileged people heavily sanctioned for next to nothing and without a commission of inquiry?

I refuse to believe in this hypothesis and above all to accept it, because in a Nation that is moreover still being built, justice and fairness in the treatment of people must prevail, as a sense of the State and a concern for preserving peace require.

Mr President of the Republic, I dare hope that this open letter, which I send you as a last resort, will retain your high attention as Head of State so that the appropriate measures may be taken by your High Authority with the sole aim of restoring the reputation of the Public Service and giving back hope to the young people who, with this competition, no longer believe in equality of opportunity in their country. It falls to you to restore the image of our country, tarnished by so many scandals and decisions at the highest level that leave one dumbfounded.

This competition, because of the seeds of frustration it has sown in hearts, must be cancelled without any form of procedure and the various perpetrators of the orchestrated schemes sanctioned with the utmost rigour, in accordance with law no. 2011-20 on the fight against corruption and other related offences in the Republic of Benin, which you promulgated on 12 October 2011 following its adoption by the National Assembly on 30 August 2011 and Decision DCC 11-064 of 30 September 2011 of the Constitutional Court on its conformity with the Constitution. Not to do so is to make a bed for uncertainty, both for the country and for all those who were selected in this competition, because the file will sooner or later be reopened.

I would therefore like to count on your high sense of responsibility, that value which is dear to me, and on your determination to mark your final term positively, to take the appropriate decisions in order to send a strong signal to the Beninese people as well as to the International Organisations that will receive a copy of this letter with the annexes.

Finally, is all this not staged to keep us occupied and to make us forget the challenges of the moment, which are named LEPI and the municipal elections? This question is put to you personally and fraternally.

Very high consideration

